.Sh DESCRIPTION
These calls were part of
.Xr libservice 3
and moved to their own library with the same names and semantics.
Each takes the process's own service context from
.Xr service_acquire 3 ;
a context inherited across a fork is refused with
.Er EINVAL .
The process-wide broker session is opened on first use, replaced
when the provider failed or was relaunched, and abandoned in a
forked child.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_open
asks the
.Dq system.Filesystem
provider
.Pq Xr BSDFilesystem 8
for the owned, type-checked storage claim named
.Fa name ,
mounts the delivered rights-limited handle, and returns its directory root in
.Fa dirfdp .
The returned descriptor anchors the store's anonymous mount: the store stays
mounted while the caller holds it
.Pq or while the provider holds the claim ,
and is unmounted when the last holder lets go, so a caller keeps it open for
as long as it uses the store and closes it before
.Fn service_storage_destroy
.Pq a mounted store cannot be destroyed .
Descriptors opened beneath it do not keep the mount alive on their own.
The caller hardens the returned directory's rights itself.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_open_cache
claims
.Fa name
in the unit's
.Em cache
sub-container
.Pq Pa Data/<bundle>/<unit>/cache/<name> :
regenerable data, reaped with the unit's container like persistent storage
but kept apart from it;
.Fn service_storage_destroy_cache
drops such a claim.
.Fn service_storage_open_shared
claims
.Fa name
in the bundle's
.Em shared
container
.Pq Pa Data/<bundle>/shared/persistent/<name> ,
reachable by every unit of the bundle and reaped with the bundle.
Several units may hold the same store at the same time: it is mounted once
and shared, and stays mounted until the last holder lets go.
.Fn service_storage_open_shared_readonly
claims the same store but delivers a
.Em read-only view :
the directory carries read-only Capsicum rights, so every descriptor derived
under it can look up, read, stat and map but never write, create, unlink,
rename or change attributes
.Pq Er ENOTCAPABLE .
A read-only claim never changes the store's ownership.
.Fn service_storage_open_env
is the conventional shared
.Em environment :
the read-only view of the shared store named
.Dv SERVICE_STORAGE_ENV
.Pq Dq env ,
which one designated unit of the bundle writes through
.Fn service_storage_open_shared
and every other unit reads.
.Fn service_storage_open_group
claims
.Fa name
in the cross-bundle
.Em group
container
.Pq Pa Data/Shared/<group>/persistent/<name> :
the unit's bundle must declare membership in
.Fa group
.Pq Bundle.ucl Va groups , see Xr switchboard 5 ,
which switchboard stamps on the connection and
.Xr BSDFilesystem 8
enforces; a non-member is refused with
.Er EPERM .
The group container is reaped only when no installed bundle claims the group
(docs/book/src/plane/containers-and-storage.md).
.Fa group
is a single safe component.
.Fn service_storage_destroy_shared
and
.Fn service_storage_destroy_group
are the symmetric
.Fn service_storage_destroy
for those scopes.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_open_quota
is
.Fn service_storage_open
with the persistent claim's
.Fa refquota
bounded to
.Fa quota
bytes;
.Fa quota
of zero selects the daemon's configured default, and a value below the daemon's
floor is rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
.Fn service_storage_open
is
.Fn service_storage_open_quota
with
.Fa quota
of zero.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_destroy
reclaims and destroys the persistent storage claim previously granted under
.Fa name ,
freeing its pool space.
The claim is resolved under the caller's own label-scoped namespace, so a
caller can never name another's;
.Er ENOENT
is returned when the named claim is absent.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_list
enumerates the caller's own persistent and cache storage claims into
.Fa claims ,
storing the number written in
.Fa countp .
The walk is scoped to the caller's own label-derived namespace, so it can only
ever return the caller's claims.
Enumeration is paged: pass
.Fa cursorp
pointing at zero for the first page; on return
.Fa *cursorp
is nonzero when more claims remain (re-issue with that value) and zero after the
last page.
A buffer of
.Dv SERVICE_STORAGE_LIST_MAX
entries always holds a whole page; a smaller
.Fa max
that cannot hold the returned page fails with
.Er EMSGSIZE ,
stores the required entry count in
.Fa *countp ,
and leaves
.Fa *cursorp
unchanged so the caller can retry without skipping claims.
Each returned
.Vt struct service_storage_claim
carries the claim's opaque
.Va name ,
its
.Va used
bytes, and its
.Va refquota
ceiling in bytes
.Pq zero when none .
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_list_shared
and
.Fn service_storage_list_group
enumerate the bundle-SHARED container and a per-group container the caller may
claim in, so a forgotten claim in either scope is discoverable to
.Fn service_storage_destroy_shared
or
.Fn service_storage_destroy_group ;
they page and owner-scope exactly like
.Fn service_storage_list .
The group variant enumerates only a group the caller's bundle declares
membership in, returning an empty page otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_stat
reports one persistent claim's live usage \(em bytes
.Va used ,
the
.Va refquota
ceiling
.Pq zero when none ,
and the bytes still
.Va available
under it \(em as an O(1) single-claim query rather than a whole-namespace walk.
It fills
.Fa out
and is owner-scoped;
.Er ENOENT
is returned when the claim is absent.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_set_quota
raises or lowers one of the caller's own persistent claims' refquota after the
mint:
.Fa quota
bytes, or zero to remove the ceiling.
The same floor and authority as the original claim apply;
.Er EINVAL
is returned below the floor and
.Er ENOENT
when the claim is absent.
.Ss Versions and transactions
A
.Em version
is a snapshot of one of the caller's own persistent claims, named by an opaque
id of at most
.Dv SERVICE_STORAGE_VERSION_MAX
bytes including the NUL.
Every version operation is owner-scoped: a caller can only ever version its own
storage.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_snapshot
snapshots
.Fa name
now and copies the assigned version id into
.Fa version .
.Fn service_storage_list_versions
pages a claim's version ids into
.Fa versions ,
with
.Fa cursorp
paging exactly as
.Fn service_storage_list
does.
.Fn service_storage_open_version
mounts
.Fa version
read-only and hands back its directory fd in
.Fa dirfdp
without disturbing the live claim; it needs an active session, and the
read-only clone is reaped with that session.
.Fn service_storage_rollback
rolls
.Fa name
back to
.Fa version ,
destroying any newer versions; it is destructive.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_txn_begin
hands back a writable staging clone of
.Fa name
\(em its directory fd in
.Fa dirfdp
\(em plus a transaction id in
.Fa txn_id .
The caller edits the clone, then
.Fn service_storage_txn_commit
atomically swaps it in over the claim, or
.Fn service_storage_txn_abort
discards it.
.Pp
Both
.Fn service_storage_txn_commit
and
.Fn service_storage_rollback
replace or rewind the live claim, so the caller must first RELEASE its mount of
that claim \(em drop the daemon-side mount anchor and close any delivered
directory fd for it \(em or the operation fails with
.Er EBUSY .
.Fn service_storage_release ,
.Fn service_storage_release_cache ,
.Fn service_storage_release_shared ,
and
.Fn service_storage_release_group
drop this process's mount anchor on a UNIT, cache, SHARED, or GROUP claim
without destroying it; they are idempotent \(em a claim not currently anchored
is a success \(em and mirror the
.Fn service_storage_open
and
.Fn service_storage_destroy
scope variants.
.Pp
.Fn service_open_config
opens this service's writable, label-scoped configuration area \(em the
well-known
.Dq config
claim under its per-service home \(em and returns its mounted directory root in
.Fa dirfdp .
It is a thin wrapper over
.Fn service_storage_open
and takes no object name.
.Pp
.Fn service_open_isolated
opens an existing filesystem
.Fa path
through
.Xr BSDFilesystem 8 Ns 's
per-label default-deny policy and returns a Capsicum-rights-limited,
close-on-exec descriptor in
.Fa fdp .
It lets a sandboxed service reach a device node, shared directory, or config
file it cannot open by path itself, with nothing declared in the manifest.
.Fa rights
is a mask of
.Dv SERVICE_OPEN_READ , SERVICE_OPEN_WRITE , SERVICE_OPEN_EXEC ,
.Dv SERVICE_OPEN_LOOKUP ,
and
.Dv SERVICE_OPEN_IOCTL ;
a non-zero
.Fa is_dir
requires that
.Fa path
be a directory.
A path the label is not granted fails with
.Er EACCES .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr BSDFilesystem 8
.Sh HISTORY
These functions appeared in
.Xr libservice 3 ;
.Nm libfilesystemcmp
appeared in 5BSD when they moved out of it.
